The three fixed criteria: MISS on P1 and P2, P3 a HIT that tested nothing. One sample; no rate is claimed.

Cause, named from the run's own transcript and logs (the second-sample rule asks for a named cause before a re-run):
  1  The loop has two doors it should continue through, and zakcode shuts both the same way. After a veto the
     hook's words lead the delivered message ("FIRST action MUST be Skill('aspirations') ... Do NOT run Bash
     commands first") although the harness has already made that call; luna makes it again, and the answer is
     "[already loaded] ... Continue those instructions from where you are; do not reload them." The other door is
     the framework's own contract -- every iteration ends on Skill(aspirations, loop) -- and mid-turn that call gets
     the same sentence. ADR-0187 counted on the paged form of that reply, which carries the current section; since
     ADR-0192 a body that fits arrives whole, there is no section to carry, and the reply is a dead end. luna reads
     it literally ("the skill loader reports it is already loaded rather than advancing the loop body"), summarises,
     ends the turn, is vetoed, and goes round: four vetoes, veto_stall, 600 s at rest, twice. The framework met this
     defect on Claude Code and wrote it down in its own Skill gate: deduplicating the loop skill's per-iteration
     re-invocation "KILLS the loop", so that gate exempts those skills.
  2  luna CAN carry a skill when the message says so. At the wake-up door the harness text reads "carry out these
     instructions from where the plan stands", and luna ran twelve entry steps in order (state gate, runner identity,
     stop and interrupt checks, loop-state restore, entry battery, compact restore, stranded-claim sweep, quiescence
     checks, digest load and read) -- then went straight to the terminal Skill call without the iteration body. It
     also ran /start and the graceful stop (twice) end to end. Why the body was skipped is NOT established by this
     sample: its blind three-step plan ended in "the required loop terminal action", and the digest it had just read
     opens with 98 lines of reference tables before its first phase.
  3  The plan-first gate refused luna's first step three times in two turns. That step was a read-only state query;
     the gate reads every shell call as mutating. The plan it forced was written before anything had been read.
  Not claimed: that fixing 1 closes an iteration. It removes the stall; what luna does with a live loop is sample 2.

