=== 2026-09-20 19:13 UTC CORRECTION -- THE FOURTH FRAMEWORK FINDING IS WITHDRAWN; STATUS OF THE OTHER THREE ===

THE CORRECTION FIRST. The 11:02 UTC block on 2026-09-19 filed a fourth framework finding: that the
framework's first-run protocol cannot be satisfied in a served context, and that a vessel started
against an unprovisioned workspace "will sit at that gate indefinitely". Both halves were wrong, and
the three blocks after it (11:53, 12:15, 13:12) each repeated the finding as open. It is WITHDRAWN.

  "Indefinitely" was false of this product. A served run is bounded by its duration cap, and since
  #571 by an optional idle window. The operator-wait tool is already refused in an unattended session
  (ADR-0133), so the turn ENDS instead of parking as awaiting -- which is what attempt 2 showed: two
  refused waits, then a completed turn. My harness would have sat until its cap, not forever.

  "Open" was false of the deployment. The production provisioner already closes this at the right
  boundary: before the served process starts it lands the framework's agent the way a transplant
  lands one -- no first-run ceremony, no human -- and it refuses to continue if that step fails. Its
  own record dates that fix to late August, three weeks before my run. Attempt 2 reached the state
  only because the world builder I wrote that morning omitted the provisioner's step; the builder has
  asserted it since attempt 3. So this was never a framework defect -- the framework is right to
  refuse to invent an identity -- and never a product defect. It was a harness defect, found, fixed
  and recorded in that same 11:02 block, and then mislabelled as somebody else's.

  HOW THE ERROR WAS MADE, since the method matters more than the instance. I filed the finding from
  what my run showed and read the provisioner AFTERWARDS. One read of the component that owns
  provisioning would have answered it. A state my own harness manufactured is evidence about my
  harness until the owning component has been read.

STATUS OF SAMPLE 4'S THREE FINDINGS, which the 13:12 UTC block left "open and unfixed". All changes
are in the framework's dev repository at 4c2c30047f and are NOT yet promoted to any other deployment.

  1. THE SELECTOR DIED WITH A BARE TRACEBACK -- FIXED, both halves. The selector now names which of
     two states it is in (never initialized on this box, or initialized with a seed file missing),
     prints the remedy, and exits with a code of its own, with no traceback and nothing on stdout.
     That alone would have been inert: the composed entry script captured the selector's stderr and
     discarded it, then parsed an empty stdout, so every failure arrived as a JSON parse error that
     named the parser and never the cause. It now checks the exit code before parsing and carries the
     selector's own reason. A fix to a producer's message does nothing until the consumer that
     composes it carries stderr and reads the exit code.

  2. CLOSING SCRIPTS ACCEPT A GOAL-LESS CLOSE -- RECLASSIFIED, deliberately NOT patched. Reading the
     closing script changed the diagnosis: its completion banner is the loop's CONTINUITY marker, not
     a success marker, and withholding it on an empty close would turn a slow loop into a dead one.
     The one candidate signal for "this iteration did no goal work" is unreliable by the script's own
     record. What sample 4 actually exposed is a detection gap, now measured on its ledger: all 3
     goal-less closes exited 0 and printed no line at all from the productivity gate, which leaves
     silently below a floor of 10 completed goals; and the loop-exhaustion fence needs a frozen
     execution diary, which the loop's own entry and close machinery keeps fresh. A second instance
     with a different cause was already on record from another box. Filed in the framework's queue
     with the measurements and NO prescribed remedy; frequency is to be measured first.

  3. RUNNING BUT NEVER INITIALIZED -- FIXED at the entry check. The loop's entry check now reports a
     missing init marker FIRST and dispatches the init step, and an unresolvable root reads as blind,
     never as a false dispatch. It ran 5 times in sample 4 and said nothing each time.

ONE NEW READING of an existing artifact -- sample 4's tool ledger, no new run and no spend. Over 140
shell calls: the init script 0, the persona write 0, the entry check 5, the composed entry script 5,
the selector 10; the boot skill loaded once. So BOTH of boot's unconditional state writes were
skipped, not one. The init half is what fix 3 nets. The persona half is not reproduced by that
dispatch, on purpose -- the marker predicate does not measure it -- and is filed as its own
observation, consequence unmeasured.

WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. Every fix above is proven by tests with mutation controls (each sabotage turned
exactly the intended tests red and the restore was byte-verified), and NONE has been measured on a
served small-model loop. Whether a small model FOLLOWS the new dispatch is the open question, and this
campaign's own record says an instruction is not a control (0 of 3 with it, 3 of 3 when the engine
binds it). That measurement needs a world that is running and never booted, and is not registered
here. Nothing about effort, terra, the pod tier or azure. No run is licensed by this block.

=== 01:32 UTC 2026-09-21 PRE-REGISTRATION -- SERVED LOOP ON LUNA, SAMPLE 6: did the rests go? Two builds that differ by ADR-0205 alone, one run each ===

WHY THIS RUN. The veto-door bench's registration 2 read GAIN for arm R (veto-door-preregistration.log, the 00:45 UTC
2026-09-21 block): with a finished plan's answer-now reminder kept silent after a refused stop, gpt-5.6-luna failed to
resume on 0 of 116 rollouts, against 18 of 116 on the baseline build. Its registered consequence was that R ships --
it did, as ADR-0205, PR #583, main c22b46f -- and that the next served sample reads whether the rests went. This is
that sample. The bench's segment ended at the first resumed command, in a synthetic world, at the FIRST refused stop
of a run. Whether a served loop then keeps running is the one thing it could not say, and the only thing asked here.

THE RESTS, as the sample-5 control measured them (the 13:12 UTC 2026-09-19 block). A served turn that ends veto_stall
is followed by a window with no model call, because the armed 600 s wake-up is the only re-entry left. On that run
three of four turns ended veto_stall and the windows were 600.30 s, 600.29 s and 302.06 s (the third closed by the
run-stop request): 1502.65 s of the 2054.25 s between launch and the run-stop request, 73.2%.

WHAT BUILDING THE READER SHOWED, from two OLD runs, before either arm has run. It is not evidence about either arm; it
changed what this block has to name. The fence that ends a turn veto_stall (ADR-0187) does not count work. It counts
refusals that name a skill, and only a Skill call answered with a BODY sets it back to zero: a call answered with the
already-loaded pointer does not, and a shell command does not. The bench's outcome was "a work call followed the
door". Those are different events, and the old traces hold them apart:
  sample-5 control  (effort none, build 60e0a8e)  11 doors: 7 stopped again in words, 2 resumed and loaded nothing,
                                                  2 loaded a skill body. 3 turns ended veto_stall.
  sample 5 attempt 3 (effort low, build 6b5a527)  17 doors: 0 stopped again in words, 9 resumed and loaded nothing,
                                                  8 loaded. 1 turn ended veto_stall -- on three doors that ALL
                                                  resumed.
So a build on which every door resumes can still end its turns veto_stall. ADR-0205 shipped for what the bench
measured, the model answering in words again. This sample can therefore show the rests staying for a reason the
bench's outcome could not see, and the reading rule names that outcome NOW rather than discovering it afterwards. The
reader checks its own reading of the fence: a turn the fence ended must show exactly three trailing doors with no body
loaded. All four such turns in the two old runs do, and no earlier run of three exists in either.

THE ARMS. Two builds of the product, one served run each, everything else the same.
  T  main c22b46f (PR #583): ADR-0205 present.                                      src tree b0ca42e66e16
  C  665e9ad, T's parent commit: ADR-0205 absent, everything else identical.         src tree 0f84ac1b4a2a
The two trees differ under src/ in ONE file, src/zakcode/agent/loop.py (+55 -1), which is ADR-0205 and nothing else.
Both carry ADR-0204, so both write each request's rails to the trace; only T writes rails_silenced. That asymmetry is
the in-trace proof of which build ran, and the reading refuses an arm whose traces do not show it.
C is the control and it is an arm of THIS batch. The sample-5 control is history, not a control for this: its build
(60e0a8e) predates four product changes that both arms carry (#571, an idle-window stop whose knob is unset here and
asserted unset; #574; ADR-0203; ADR-0204), it ran two days ago, and it did not select its build through PYTHONPATH.
Its numbers are quoted for scale and decide nothing.

THE ASSEMBLY is the sample-5 control's, rebuilt by the same script from the same seed: seed 2.12.76 (sha256 prefix
71c88a26f3b953c0), the provisioning step and its assertions, the same sandbox, ceremony, caps (2400 s run, 350 s
reserve, 2520 s supervisor wall clock, $6.00 supervisor budget per run), trace directory and claim-endpoint patch
(sha256 prefix 74b5eca7c44d0189); no reasoning_effort configured, so the product's forced none applies (ADR-0200).
run_served.sh, progress.py and served.env are byte-identical to the control world's copies, compared by hash today and
asserted again by the launcher.
  FOUND AND REPAIRED BEFORE THIS BLOCK WAS WRITTEN. The scratch world builder's header said the claim patch is
  re-applied and its body never did it: the sample-5 worlds and the control each got the patched file from a separate
  copy run by hand after the build. Both fresh worlds came up with the seed's UNPATCHED endpoint (prefix
