  After the run, from the artifacts:
  G6  the run's first progress line names the arm's tree as the zakcode source.
  G7  the traces carry the build's signature: every usage event has rails; T's carry rails_silenced and C's do not.

WHAT IS READ, and from where. Two readers, committed WITH this block so the rule below can be re-run by anyone, and
both checked against known answers before it (MANIFEST at the foot of this block).
  rests  (serve.log, progress.log) A WINDOW opens at a turn-ended line and closes at the next turn's boot line or at
         the run-stop request, whichever is first. Nothing after the run-stop request counts: that is teardown. Run
         length is launch to the run-stop request. It reproduces the control block's figures exactly: 600.30 / 600.29
         / 302.06 s, 1502.65 of 2054.25 s. It does NOT reproduce attempt 3's "733 s of 2194 s": that block measured to
         a later end point by an older method. Attempt 3's one window, 600.16 s, is reproduced. Only the control
         block's definition is used here.
  doors  (per-turn traces) A DOOR is an honoured refusal, marked by the harness's delivery note. Its segment runs to
         the next door or the end of the turn. It is a FINISHED-PLAN door (F) if the first request after it names
         plan_complete in rails (the line was SENT) or in rails_silenced (the line was SILENT); otherwise an OTHER
         door (o). The segment STOPPED AGAIN IN WORDS (-) if it holds no work call, by the product's own definition of
         one (its three tool sets are imported from the product, not re-typed); it RESUMED (+) if it holds one; it
         LOADED (*) if it holds a Skill call answered with a body. 10 known-answer checks pass. A delivery cannot
         reopen the plan here: a skill body that fits the window seeds no plan steps (ADR-0192), and the control's
         traces hold 0 seeding notes.
  A stall turn's TRAILING DOORS are its last three: the ones the fence counted.

THE READING RULE, over every outcome, fixed before launch. V_C and V_T are the turns that ended veto_stall before the
run-stop request, in arm C and arm T.
  ARM NOT READ        any gate fails for that arm, or its run never reaches a refused stop (no door at all: a crash, a
                      failover, a provider outage, a budget stop). Nothing is concluded from that arm.
  NOT DISCRIMINATING  C is read and V_C = 0. There were no rests to remove. T is not launched (the spend gate above).
  RESTS GONE          both arms read, V_C >= 1 and V_T = 0.
                      It is ATTRIBUTED to ADR-0205 only if C shows the mechanism live where it stalled -- at least one
                      of C's trailing doors is an F door with the line SENT that stopped again in words -- and every F
                      door in T shows the line SILENT. Otherwise it is RESTS GONE, UNATTRIBUTED.
                      Either way it is one run. Six earlier served runs, on different builds and depths, ended 0, 1,
                      2, 2, 3 and 3 turns veto_stall, so a single run with 0 is something the baseline has done
                      itself.
  RESTS REMAIN        both arms read, V_C >= 1 and V_T >= 1. Fewer stall turns in T than in C is RESTS REMAIN and is
                      NOT read as a reduction: the baseline's own spread covers any such difference at one run per
                      arm. What the fence counted in T is then reported as a partition of T's trailing doors, each of
                      which is exactly one of:
                        W  an F door, line silent, that stopped again in words: the bench's gain did not carry to
                           these doors (a later delivery, a longer conversation);
                        O  an o door that stopped again in words: a door the bench never captured;
                        N  any door that RESUMED and loaded no skill body: the model went back to work and the fence
                           still counted it, so the bench's outcome was the wrong proxy for a served stall;
                        U  no request followed, or the door cannot be classified. An F door in T whose line was
                           SENT belongs here too: ADR-0205 says it cannot happen, and it is reported as a fault.
                      The largest class is named as what is left; a tie is reported as a tie. That describes WHICH
                      doors the fence counted. It is not a cause: nothing here separates why a door went as it did.
  Rule E stands: an outcome nobody named is reported as measured and never upgraded. NOTHING SHIPS OR UN-SHIPS from
  this sample in any branch. ADR-0205 stands on the bench's registered rule and on its own argument -- a harness must
  not talk over the hook that just refused the stop -- whatever this reads.

WHAT EACH READING WOULD CHANGE, which is why it is worth its cost. RESTS GONE, after its one licensed replication: the
veto door closes as a served blocker for luna, and the next served work is the iteration body, which has closed 0
goals in six runs. RESTS REMAIN / W: the next door bench forks at a LATER delivery, not the first. RESTS REMAIN / O:
the next door bench captures open-plan doors. RESTS REMAIN / N: the next bench's outcome becomes "a skill body loaded
before the next stop", and the fence's own definition is re-read against what those segments actually did.

ESTIMATES, recorded before launch so that each can be scored.
  C is read                                              85%
  V_C >= 1, given C is read                              80%
  T is read, given it is launched                        85%
  V_T = 0, given both read and V_C >= 1                  20%   I held about 35% before reading the old traces. A
                                                               resumed door does not reset the fence, and at effort
                                                               low 9 of 17 resumed doors loaded nothing.
  share of C's doors that are F doors                    65%   I do not know this. No served trace has ever shown it.
  F doors that stopped again in words                    C (line sent) 55%;  T (line silent) 20%
  if RESTS REMAIN, the largest class in T                N 45%, W 35%, O 15%, a tie or U 5%

SECONDARY MEASURES, reported for both arms and deciding nothing: idle seconds and idle share; the door table (F and o,
by -, + and *); T's count of refusals taken on a finished plan (its own note) beside T's count of F doors, which must
agree unless something reopened the plan between the refusal and the next request; turns, iterations, calls, cost and
cached share; goals completed; plan share of the tool ledger.

SECOND-SAMPLE RULE. ARM NOT READ licenses one re-run of THAT arm, in a fresh world at the same settings. RESTS GONE
licenses ONE replication of T alone in a fresh world: two runs at 0 reads "RESTS GONE, replicated once", and otherwise
RESTS REMAIN stands on the run that stalled. NOT DISCRIMINATING licenses one more pair, C first again so that the
spend gate holds -- which leaves run order tied to the arm, a confound accepted here and named -- and a second NOT
DISCRIMINATING ends the question as unanswerable at this price rather than buying a third. RESTS REMAIN licenses
nothing: the partition is the result, and the next step is a bench, not another served run.

WHAT THIS CANNOT SETTLE, whatever it reads. Anything about a model other than gpt-5.6-luna at effort none. Whether a
loop that keeps running closes goals. WHY a door went the way it did: the doors of one run share a conversation, so
they are not independent observations, the unit is the run, and n is 1 per arm. A test on the door table would be
decoration and none will be reported. Whether the fence's definition is right.

SPEND. The supervisor bounds each run at $6.00 and 2520 s, as in every earlier sample. Expected: about $1 for C (the
control cost $0.83 with 73% of its wall clock idle) and $1 to $3.3 for T: a run with no rests calls the model for the
whole window, and at the control's rate while active ($0.83 over 552 s) a fully active run is about $3.1. The bound is
deliberately NOT lowered to cap the total. A budget stop in T would end its run early for a reason that is neither a
rest nor a stall, in exactly the branch this sample exists to read. Campaign spend so far: about $9.59. No other model
call is made while a run is in flight, and the arms' worktrees are not touched until both runs are read.

